# ── Chapter 8 fidelity pins: "What You Are Owed" ─────────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/08-what-you-are-owed.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# NOTE — one absence this file cannot hold as a query, and it is load-bearing.
# "The general floor debt still has no machinery that checks performance against
# arrival" rests on nothing anywhere READING `owe`. The obligations family gives
# other duties typed consumers without turning the legacy debt into delivery.
# Test the rule BODY, left of the arrow — a plain `grep owe` matches Article 1b's
# own head and can never fail.
#
# The `:require` lines need `--allow-shell`, and both commands above pass it. The
# older per-file command did not, and its failure was doubly misleading: the
# skipped preconditions were not counted, so it reported a pin-count mismatch —
# "pins were added or lost" — as though somebody had edited this file. If you ever
# see that, check the flag before you go looking for a missing pin.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 51

:require test -z "$(awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /(^|[^[:alnum:]_])owe[[:space:]]*\(/ {print}' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)"
:require test -n "$(awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /false/ {print}' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)"

# "the answer this design gives is no ... for every one of the eight"
? eats(Adam).
# => FALSE

? healthy(Bela).
# => FALSE

? secure(Bela).
# => FALSE

? learn(Cira).
# => FALSE

? believe(Bela).
# => FALSE

? meets(Hano).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Bela).
# => FALSE

# "Shelter derives for every confined person and for nobody else." Both halves are
# pinned, and the second half is the one that carries the discomfort: the design
# is certain about the housing of exactly the people it is punishing. Kel and
# Adam are the v0.8 cases — homeless, and housed by being confined.
? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? dwell(Kel).
# => TRUE

? dwell(Adam).
# => TRUE

# ...and for nobody else. Bela above is voided and not confined; Cira is a child
# who was never convicted of anything.
? dwell(Cira).
# => FALSE

# THE SECOND EXCEPTION, same width exactly. "Recorded speech derives the same
# way: for every confined person and for nobody else." This entry was the one
# the old universal hid — this file pinned the "no" for every floor entry
# EXCEPT recorded speech, so the one entry that answers TRUE was the one
# nothing looked at. Hano carries the confined half; Bela (voided, free) and
# Jala (never accused — chapter 13's control) carry "nobody else".
? expresses(Hano).
# => TRUE

? expresses(Bela).
# => FALSE

? expresses(Jala).
# => FALSE

# "Your credibility is still reachable."
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~believe($x) -> false($x).

# "Your recognition is still reachable."
# [v0.8] NOTE this control is precisely the shape INVARIANT 1 now forbids in the
# constitution: a floor right read into a consequence rather than into `err`. It
# stays here, because the point of the control is that the ENGINE permits it —
# which is exactly why the invariant has to, and now does, refuse it separately.
# Scoped, and that is not cosmetic here: `verify.sh` reads INVARIANT 1 off the
# constitution and never off a pin file, so under a plain `:accept` the forbidden
# shape stayed resident and every pin below it in this file ran against a base
# that broke the invariant — measured 2026-08-01, `lose(Points, Hano)` derived,
# Hano being confined with `meets` FALSE for him, pinned above. The widening
# stopped at the file boundary: measured the same day, nibli-pin rebuilds the base
# per file, so a plain `:accept` here was never visible to the next file's pins.
# And this is not the only site it applied to — the credibility control above
# reads a floor right into `false`, which is the same forbidden shape. This is
# where the invariant gets named, not where it alone bit. Showing that the engine
# permits the shape must not mean leaving it enacted.
:accept-scoped
all $x: person($x) & ~meets($x) -> lose(Points, $x).

# "And nothing prevents compulsion." — the floor blocks absence, not manufacture
:accept-scoped
all $x: prisoner($x) -> believe($x).

# Every `:accept` above is scoped: the statement must compile and load, that
# loadability is the pin verdict, and the statement is then retracted. So the
# controls no longer widen the base, and a query may be added at this point.
# Scoping the controls is not the same as the file never widening its own base,
# though — the `judge`/`capture` assertions further down are ordinary ground
# facts, unscoped and resident from the point they are written, so ordering
# still matters for anything added below them.
#
# HISTORY, and why the scoping is not cosmetic here. These were plain `:accept`,
# which left each rule in the KB, and this file's defence was an argument rather
# than a mechanism: `owe` carries no `~false` guard and `authority`/`permits`
# read `false` nowhere, so the queries below were sound — but only for as long as
# that stayed true of whatever got added next. Measured under the old form: at
# this line `lose(Points, Hano)` came back TRUE, so the recognition control noted
# above had already fired on an absent floor right and everything below was being
# checked against a base that broke INVARIANT 1. Under the scoped form it is
# FALSE. Hano is the exhibit and Bela is not: `lose(Points, Bela)` is TRUE with
# no accept at all, since Bela is voided and voiding claws points back unaided.

# ── "Owed by whom" ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "owed by a public body ... whose obligation runs to every person — not to
#  members, not to contributors, not to residents, not to anyone who has
#  qualified for it"
? owe(State, Provision, Bela).
# => TRUE

# scoped to persons and to nothing else: no roster entry, nothing owed
? owe(State, Provision, Sokk).
# => FALSE

# [v0.8] "The record names each of the things owed ... and owes each of them to
#  every person." The token survives beside them and is pinned above; these say
#  the debt now has content.
? owe(State, Eats, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Meets, Hano).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Believe, Cira).
# => TRUE

# same scoping as the token — a non-person is owed nothing, itemised or not
? owe(State, Eats, Sokk).
# => FALSE

# "The entry naming food and the question of whether anyone ate are separate
#  things in this design, and nothing joins them." Bela is owed food, and shelter,
#  and has neither. If either of these ever flips while the owe pins stay TRUE,
#  something joined them and the passage above is wrong.
# "Not whether Bela is *entitled* to eat — whether Bela eats." The chapter's
# whole opening turns on this contrast, and until now the file pinned only the
# second half. Both halves, adjacent, same person:
? entitled(Bela, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? eats(Bela).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Bela).
# => FALSE

# ── OWED IN FULL, ENUMERATED, ONE PERSON ─────────────────────────────────────
# "a statement of what is owed to every person, in full" and "the record names
# each of the things owed ... and owes each of them to every person" — checked
# in full on the chapter's own person, every item on both sides of the debt,
# not sampled. An enumeration is the only shape under which "every one" is a
# checked phrase rather than a summary, because the omitted item is exactly
# where such claims fail — this file's own expresses pin was missing for as
# long as the prose's false universal hid behind it.
? entitled(Bela, event { secure() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { dwell() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { healthy() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { learn() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { expresses() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { believe() }).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Bela, event { meets() }).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Secure, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Dwell, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Healthy, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Learn, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Expresses, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Believe, Bela).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Meets, Bela).
# => TRUE

# "the body can be looked at"
? authority(State).
# => TRUE

# "it was never given the credential that makes an examination count — so it can
#  write down what it likes about you and none of it will void your credibility"
# BOTH halves are pinned, because the first draft of this sentence said the body
# "holds no power to examine anyone" and that is FALSE: `judge` and `capture`
# have no precondition, so anybody may record an examination of anybody. What
# the body lacks is the credential that gives a recorded examination force.
judge(State, Bela).
capture(State, Bela).
? capture(State, Bela).
# => TRUE

? permits(Review, State).
# => FALSE

# "No law can be written that puts a class of people outside the provision and
#  then punishes them for being outside it." The `~home` control in
#  rights-floor.pins.nibli is what proves this is the obligation doing it and
#  not the shape of the rule.
:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'owe'/
all $x: person($x) & ~owe(State, Provision, $x) -> prisoner($x).

# [v0.8] The protection came with the itemisation and was not re-argued for it.
# Same refusal on a NAMED debt: no law may put a class outside the food debt and
# then jail them for being outside it. Pinned because "it extends to each of them"
# is the kind of claim that is assumed rather than checked.
:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'owe'/
all $x: person($x) & ~owe(State, Eats, $x) -> prisoner($x).

# ── [v0.9 2026-08-02] THE LEGACY LEARNING ROUTE, DORMANT. Ground fact persists ─
# Teaching that was heard derives learning. It was the first non-carceral route;
# the later delivery-receipt family adds separately pinned recipient-side routes
# for other floor actualities without changing this legacy shape. The shipped
# record holds no hearing, so this route remains dormant; dormant is not blind,
# and this pair is the proof. The arrival is two-sided (the teaching is on the
# record above; the hearing is written here) and it rates nobody: Cira learns,
# and Cira still earns nothing for being taught — delivery is floor, not
# recognition, in both directions at once.
? learn(Cira).
# => FALSE

hears(Cira, Bela).

? learn(Cira).
# => TRUE

? reward(Cira).
# => FALSE
